<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alcmaeonidae</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Alcmaeonids</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Alcmaeonids were a powerful noble family of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. They were accused of sacrilege for killing followers of Cylon in a sanctuary around 632 BCE, and spent periods in exile. During one such exile, after a fire in 548 BCE, they rebuilt the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> at Delphi with a marble façade, winning the oracle's favour. Their members included the reformer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleisthenes">Cleisthenes</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a> was an Alcmaeonid through his mother.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alcmaeonidae.jpg" alt="Alcmaeonidae" width="320" height="534" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographer · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greece_from_the_Coming_of_the_Hellenes_to_AD._14,_page_137,_Pericles.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Alcmaeonids were a wealthy and powerful noble family of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, a branch of the Neleides who claimed descent from the mythological Alcmaeon, the great-grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nestor">Nestor</a>. Unlike many aristocratic families, they were named after a historical figure, Alcmaeon, the father of Megacles. They reused the names of grandfathers, so that Megacles, Alcmaeon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleisthenes">Cleisthenes</a> recur throughout the family. The first noteworthy member was Megacles, son of Alcmaeon, who was Archon Eponymous of Athens in the 7th century BCE. Their main aristocratic rivals in the 6th and 5th centuries BCE were the Peisistratids.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>As chief archon at the time of the Cylonian affair, around 632 BCE, Megacles had the followers of Cylon killed after they left the sanctuary where they had sought refuge, having promised them safety. This broke the sacred law protecting suppliants, and a curse was believed to cling to Megacles and his descendants. The family was exiled, and buried Alcmaeonid bodies were dug up and removed from the city. Later, Alcmaeon served as an intermediary between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croesus">Croesus of Lydia</a> and the oracle at Delphi and was allowed to take as much gold as he could carry, which gave the family wealth and a path back to prominence.</p><p>Another Megacles married his daughter to the tyrant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peisistratus">Pisistratus</a>, but, according to the account, Pisistratus refused to have children with her and Megacles and his allies banished him. Megacles later married Agarista, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleisthenes-of-sicyon">Cleisthenes of Sicyon</a>. Their sons were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippocrates">Hippocrates</a> and a second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleisthenes">Cleisthenes</a>, the reformer, while Hippocrates' daughter Agariste was the mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a>. In 508 BCE the reformer Cleisthenes overthrew Hippias, the son of Pisistratus, with Spartan help obtained from the oracle at Delphi. He was opposed because of the curse and briefly exiled, but the citizens called him back. His reforms replaced the four traditional tribes with ten based on residence, created a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boule-ancient-greece">Boule</a> of 500 members, introduced sortition and established ostracism.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> reports an accusation that the Alcmaeonids signalled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> with a shield during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-persian-wars">Persian Wars</a>, a story he himself refused to believe and which scholars debate. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a> the Spartans cited the family's curse to discredit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a>, who led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> from roughly 461 to 429 BCE and began the building programme that produced most surviving structures on the Acropolis, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthenon">Parthenon</a>. Alcibiades, an orator, general and statesman, also belonged to the family and changed sides several times in the war. He was the last notable Alcmaeonid, and the family disappeared from prominence after the defeat of Athens.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alcmaeonidae” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The tyrant Cleisthenes, early 6th century BCE, renamed the Dorian tribes, fought Argos and helped win the First Sacred War; his daughter Agariste married into the Athenian <b>Alcmaeonids</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/sicyon">Sicyon</a></li></ul></section>
